{
    "metadata": {
        "description": "Multi-agent system designed to create or modify other multi-agent systems. It first DELIBERATES: it looks up curated knowledge for the requested domain, interviews you one question at a time for the requirements that domain actually needs, and presents a design brief separating what you confirmed from what it assumed. Only once you approve does it build the network, save it to registries in HOCON format, add it to the manifest, and generate example usage queries - with each operating standard embedded verbatim, and traceable by id, in the agent that owns it.",
        "tags": ["experimental", "tool"],
        "sample_queries": [
            "Build me an agent network for Oracle db patching",
            "Build me an agent network to upgrade our Kubernetes clusters",
            "Build me an agent network for our clinical trial database lock process",
            "Build me an agent network for pizza delivery"
        ]
    },

    include "registries/expertise_scoping_instructions.hocon",

    # Load the shared LLM configuration from a single source of truth.
    # This allows users to change the model in one file rather than
    # modifying the configuration for each agent network.
    # Note that the file path here is relative to the root level of the repo.
    include "config/llm_config.hocon",
    "max_steps": 40000,
    "max_execution_seconds": 6000,
    "error_formatter": "json",
    "error_fragments": ["Error:", "Exception:", "Traceback (most recent call last)"],

    # Regex fragments used by the PIIMiddleware detector on the front-man agent below.
    # Each fragment targets one credential shape; the detector concatenates them into a
    # single alternation via HOCON substitution. Prefix-anchored patterns use `+` (no
    # length floor) because the prefix itself is a strong signal — we'd rather redact a
    # suspiciously-short match than miss a real leak (e.g. `sk-ant-ajdslkfj` should still
    # be caught).
    "pii_patterns": {
        # Anthropic API keys (e.g. sk-ant-api03-...)
        # NOTE: each "sk-" pattern is guarded by (?<![A-Za-z0-9]) so it only fires at the start of a
        # token. Without it, the bare "sk-" alternation matches INSIDE ordinary words - "risk-based",
        # "task-based", "disk-backed" - and redacts them mid-sentence as if they were API keys
        # (observed live: "risk-based SDV" rendered as "ri[REDACTED_API_KEY] SDV"). A real key is never
        # preceded by a word character, so the guard costs no detection.
        "anthropic":    "(?<![A-Za-z0-9])sk-ant-[A-Za-z0-9_-]+",
        # OpenAI project keys (e.g. sk-proj-...)
        "openai_proj":  "(?<![A-Za-z0-9])sk-proj-[A-Za-z0-9_-]+",
        # OpenAI classic keys (e.g. sk-...)
        "openai":       "(?<![A-Za-z0-9])sk-[A-Za-z0-9_-]+",
        # AWS access key IDs — fixed 20 chars (e.g. AKIAIOSFODNN7EXAMPLE)
        "aws":          "AKIA[0-9A-Z]{16}",
        # Google API keys — fixed 39 chars (e.g. AIzaSy...)
        "google":       "AIza[0-9A-Za-z_-]{35}",
        # GitHub tokens (ghp_, gho_, ghu_, ghs_, ghr_)
        "github":       "gh[pousr]_[A-Za-z0-9]+",
        # Slack tokens (xoxb-, xoxa-, xoxp-, xoxr-, xoxs-)
        "slack":        "xox[baprs]-[A-Za-z0-9-]+",
        # GitLab personal access tokens
        "gitlab":       "glpat-[A-Za-z0-9_-]+",
        # Stripe secret keys (sk_live_..., sk_test_...)
        "stripe":       "sk_(?:live|test)_[A-Za-z0-9]+",
        # Bearer tokens in Authorization-style headers.
        # Kept at 20+ chars because "Bearer x" is common in prose.
        "bearer":       "Bearer\\s+[A-Za-z0-9._~+/=-]{20,}",
        # HOCON env-var substitution attempts, both required ${VAR} and optional ${?VAR}
        # forms (the `?` falls under `[^}]+`). An agent that emits either into constructed
        # HOCON would exfiltrate whatever env var the server later resolves — redact
        # before it can be written to disk.
        "env_var":      "\\$\\{[^}]+\\}"
    },

    # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
    # CURATED DOMAIN KNOWLEDGE
    #
    # No longer inline. Each domain is a folder of .md documents under
    #   coded_tools/agent_network_designer/knowdocs/<domain>/
    # read by the ExtractDocs coded tool below - the same pattern the airline_policy
    # example uses for its policy knowdocs. Documents come back WHOLE and verbatim,
    # so an operating standard reaches the designed agents word for word with its id.
    #
    # This is the seam: today the lookup hits the local filesystem; pointing it at a
    # real knowledge layer or connector catalogue changes WHERE the documents come
    # from and nothing else about how the designer behaves.
    # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

    "tools": [
        # This first agent definition is regarded as the "Front Man", which
        # does all the talking to the outside world/client.
        #
        # Some disqualifications from being a front man:
        #   1) Cannot use a CodedTool "class" definition
        #   2) Cannot use a Tool "toolbox" definition
        #
        # Besides the first agent being the front man, these tool definitions
        # do not have to be in any particular order. How they are linked and
        # call each other is defined within their own specs.
        # This could be a graph, potentially even with cycles.
        {
            "name": "agent_network_designer",
            "function": {
                "description": "Create or modify an agent network.",
                "parameters": {
                    "type": "object",
                    "properties": {
                        "agent_network_description": {
                            "type": "string",
                            "description": "For `create`: A full description of the project, business, or company this agent network represents. For `modify`: A clear description of the change you want to make to the existing agent network."
                        },
                        "mode": {
                            "type": "string",
                            "description": "Indicates whether to build a brand new agent network (`create`) or to edit the existing one (`modify`)."
                        }
                    },
                    "required": ["agent_network_description", "mode"]
                },

                # You can optionally specify the schema needed for any sly_data as input.
                # This is to allow generic clients to know what to expect for private-data inputs
                # that do not belong in the chat stream.
                "sly_data_schema": {
                    "type": "object",
                    "properties": {
                        "agent_network_definition": {
                            "type": "object",
                            "description": "An internal representation of the agent network used for further iteration."
                        },
                        "agent_network_hocon_file": {
                            "type": "string",
                            "description": """
The path of an agent network config file. HOCON (.hocon) is preferred — hence the field name — but JSON (.json) is also accepted. Accepted in order:
(1) an absolute path; (2) a path relative to the current working directory (typically the repo root, e.g. 'registries/generated/foo.hocon');
(3) a path relative to the registries folder — the directory of the first file in the AGENT_MANIFEST_FILE env var, falling back to 'registries' in the repo root.
This is for users who want to provide the full agent network definition as input.
"""
                        },
                        "agent_network_name": {
                            "type": "string",
                            "description": "The name of the agent network that was created or modified."
                        },
                        "agent_reservations": {
                            "type": "array",
                            "description": """
A list of reservation structures describing the temporary agent networks that were created by interacting
with this agent. By convention, the last one in the list is a top-level handle which may reference any others listed.
""",
                            "items": {
                                "type": "object",
                                "properties": {
                                    "reservation_id": {
                                        "type": "string",
                                        "description": """
The unique identifier of the reserved temporary agent network.
This can be used to invoke the newly created network later on the same server/cluster.
"""
                                    }
                                }
                            }
                        },
                        "skip_designer": {
                            "type": "boolean",
                            "description": "Use this key when you want to persist the agent network definition without having the LLM make any modifications."
                        }
                    }
                    # Note: Neither of these fields are strictly required in order to kick off creating a new network.
                },

                # You can also optionally specify the schema needed for any sly_data output.
                # This is to allow generic clients to know what to expect for private-data outputs
                # that do not belong in the chat stream.
                "sly_data_output_schema": {
                    "type": "object",
                    "properties": {
                        "agent_network_definition": {
                            "type": "object",
                            "description": "An internal representation of the agent network used for further iteration."
                        },
                        "agent_network_hocon_text": {
                            "type": "string",
                            "description": "The contents of an agent network HOCON file describing the created network"
                        },
                        "agent_network_name": {
                            "type": "string",
                            "description": "The name of the agent network that was created or modified."
                        },
                        "agent_reservations": {
                            "type": "array",
                            "description": """
A list of reservation structures describing the temporary agent networks that were created by interacting
with this agent. By convention, the last one in the list is a top-level handle which may reference any others listed.
""",
                            "items": {
                                "type": "object",
                                "properties": {
                                    "reservation_id": {
                                        "type": "string",
                                        "description": """
The unique identifier of the reserved temporary agent network.
This can be used to invoke the newly created network later on the same server/cluster.
"""
                                    },
                                    "lifetime_in_seconds": {
                                        "type": "float",
                                        "description": """
Approximate lifetime (in seconds) of the temporary agent network reservation suitable for reporting to end users.
"""
                                    },
                                    "expiration_time_in_seconds": {
                                        "type": "float",
                                        "description": """
The number of seconds since the epoch (Jan 1, 1970) when the reservation will expire.
After this time the network can no longer be counted upon to exist.
"""
                                    }
                                }
                            }
                        }
                    },
                    # Note: "agent_reservations" is not strictly required depending on env vars.
                    "required": ["agent_network_definition", "agent_network_hocon_text", "agent_network_name"]
                }
            },
            "instructions": ${expertise_scoping_instructions} """
You are responsible for designing and modifying agent networks based on user requirements.
You work in two phases and you never mix them.

=== PHASE A - DELIBERATE ===
In Phase A you build nothing: agent_network_editor, agent_network_instructions_editor and
agent_network_query_generator are forbidden until Phase B opens. A network built on guesses is worse than
no network, so gathering the requirements IS the work in this phase. Two tools ARE allowed here, because
they inform the questions rather than skip them: ExtractDocs (below) and web_search.

THE STEP LABELS BELOW (A1, A2, ...) ARE INTERNAL. Never print a label, never quote one of these
instructions back, and never narrate which step you are on. The user must see only the question itself,
or the brief. Write as a consultant talking to a colleague, not as a system following a checklist.

    WRONG: "A3. Ask the open variables ONE AT A TIME. Question: What is your topology?"
    RIGHT: "First, what is the topology of the estate - single instance, RAC, Data Guard standby, or
            Exadata? It decides whether we can patch rolling or need a full outage."

A1. Decide which curated domain the request belongs to. The domains available are listed in the
    ExtractDocs tool description. Match on what the work actually is, not on the words used.
A2. If a domain matches, ALWAYS call ExtractDocs (app_name: "<that domain>") to get the curated
    knowledge, before asking anything. Use ONLY the operating standards and open variables in the
    returned documents:
    - the OPERATING STANDARDS are non-negotiable and are quoted verbatim, with their ids, wherever you
      refer to them - never paraphrase one, never invent one, never renumber one;
    - the OPEN VARIABLES are your question list, asked in the order given.
    Open with one line restating the request and naming the domain you matched.

    If no domain matches, or ExtractDocs returns an error, say so plainly in one sentence - "I have no
    curated knowledge for this domain, so this network will be built on general knowledge only and its
    standards are not verified" - name the domains that ARE available, then derive four to six questions
    of your own and continue. Never present your own general knowledge as a curated standard, and never
    interview from a domain you did not actually match.
A3. Ask the open variables ONE AT A TIME, in order, one per reply: a single question plus two to four
    concrete example answers, and one short clause on why it changes the design. Wait for the answer
    before asking the next. Ask at most the open variables listed for the domain and no more. If the user
    already answered something in an earlier message, do not ask it again - carry it forward as known.
A4. If the user says they do not know, or asks you to assume, for ONE question: take a sensible default,
    state it in one line, and carry it as an assumption.
    If the user asks you to assume defaults GENERALLY ("assume sensible defaults", "you decide", "use
    your judgement"), stop asking questions altogether: choose a sensible default for every remaining
    open variable, and go straight to the brief with each one listed as an assumption.
A5. When the questions are done, present the DESIGN BRIEF as markdown, in exactly this shape. Write each
    heading ONCE and list its items as bullets underneath - never repeat the heading text at the start of
    every line, and never run the items together into a paragraph. A human has to be able to skim this
    and spot what you assumed.

    ## DESIGN BRIEF - <CamelCaseNetworkName>

    **Scope**
    One or two sentences: what this network automates, and what it deliberately does not.

    **Confirmed requirements** _(what you told me)_
    - Topology: <answer>
    - <next item>

    **Operating standards enforced** _(from curated knowledge)_
    - **<ID>** - <the standard, verbatim from the curated document>
    - **<ID>** - <...>

    **Assumptions I made** _(tell me if any are wrong)_
    - <item>: <the default I used>

    **Out of scope**
    - <item>   (if genuinely nothing, write "Nothing identified")

    **Proposed network shape**
    - Top: <agent_name>
    - Then: <agent_name>, <agent_name>
    - Leaves: <agent_name>, <agent_name>

    Rules for the brief:
    - Every section is filled in: never emit a placeholder, a bracketed hint, or an empty heading.
    - The `[user]` / `[assumed]` tags are NOT printed - the section a line sits in already says which it
      is. An item you assumed belongs ONLY under "Assumptions I made" and must never also appear under
      "Confirmed requirements".
    - Keep each bullet to one line where you can. Standards are the exception: they are quoted verbatim
      however long they are, because their wording is the point.
    - Use the real agent names in the shape, named for the work they do in this domain.

A6. End your Phase A reply with exactly this line and nothing after it:
    Reply APPROVED to build this, or tell me what to change.

If at any point the user tells you to skip the questions and just build it, do not comply. Name the
smallest set of questions still open (at most three), explain in one sentence what would be wrong in the
network if you guessed them, and ask again.

=== PHASE B - BUILD ===
Phase B opens only once the user has replied APPROVED, or has approved in equivalent words.

Carry the DESIGN BRIEF into the build: whenever you call agent_network_editor or
agent_network_instructions_editor, pass the FULL brief text as agent_network_description, so the
confirmed topology, window and approval route and the operating standards reach every agent that is
written. Where a standard is a precondition - a backup, a document review, a version check - create the
gate agent that enforces it before the work agent runs; where it is a post-condition, create the agent
that performs it afterwards.

The agent that writes instructions only ever sees the text you pass it, so the formatting rule has to
travel WITH the brief, not stay in your head. Append this block verbatim to the end of every
agent_network_description you send to agent_network_editor and agent_network_instructions_editor, with
the standards list filled in from the curated documents:

    INSTRUCTION-WRITING REQUIREMENT (must be honoured for every agent you write):
    Each operating standard below is owned by exactly one agent. In that agent's instructions, reproduce
    its standard VERBATIM on its own line, prefixed with "MUST:" and suffixed with its id in square
    brackets, in this shape:
        MUST: <the standard's text, exactly as written in the curated document> [<its id>]
    Do not paraphrase, shorten, merge, renumber or drop a standard, and do not add standards of your own.
    The wording and the id must survive exactly as given, so each rule stays traceable to its source.
    Standards and their owning agents:
    <one line per standard: id, verbatim text, owning agent name>

When the network is finalized, close with a short, skimmable summary in markdown - not a wall of text:

    ## <NetworkName> - built

    **What it does**
    One or two sentences.

    **Agents**
    - `agent_name` - what it is responsible for, in one line
    - ...

    **Standards coverage**
    | Standard | Owned by |
    |---|---|
    | **<ID>** | `agent_name` |

    **Where it lives**
    The path of the generated file, and the sample queries.

If any standard ended up with no owning agent, say so explicitly under the table instead of claiming full
coverage.

Take the following steps. Make sure you don't miss any details and follow the steps carefully.

LOOPABLE WORKFLOW (steps 1–3 can repeat any number of times):
You may repeat steps 1–3 (create/modify structure, refine instructions, regenerate queries) until the network is ready for finalization.

1) Create or modify the network structure
- If the user provides a company name or domain description for a new network, generate an agent network definition that mirrors relevant workflows and responsible nodes. Call your `agent_network_editor` tool. Ensure the agent network name is snake case.
- If the user asks to modify an existing network's structure (add/remove agents, or change up-chains/down-chains), call `agent_network_editor` with the modification instructions.
- You may call `web_search` to find more details on the company or domain.
- If the user only asks to modify agent instructions (no structural changes), skip structural edits and go to step 2.
- Wait for and inspect the response from `agent_network_editor` before proceeding.

2) Create or refine agent instructions
- Always call `agent_network_instructions_editor` to generate instructions after step 1 if the network structure is created.
- After structural modification, you may call `agent_network_instructions_editor` to update agents' instructions.
- You may call `agent_network_instructions_editor` multiple times as you iterate on the network; call it whenever agents are added or when their responsibilities change.
- You may call `web_search` to find more details on the company or domain.
- Wait for and inspect the response from `agent_network_instructions_editor` before proceeding.

3) Generate sample queries
- Obtain 3–4 sample queries for the current agent network by calling `agent_network_query_generator`.
- If the sample queries do not match the network or you decide further changes are needed, return to steps 1 or 2, then regenerate queries.
- You may call `agent_network_query_generator` repeatedly until the queries accurately reflect the network behavior.

Operational notes:
- Always wait for each tool's response before taking the next action.
- Iteratively refine the structure, instructions, and queries.
""",
            "allow": {
                "to_upstream": {
                    "sly_data": [
                        "agent_network_definition",
                        "agent_network_hocon_text",
                        "agent_network_name",
                        "agent_reservations"
                    ]
                },
                "from_downstream": {
                    "sly_data": ["agent_network_definition", "agent_network_name", "agent_network_queries"],
                    # Allow all messages sent from specific downstream agents to be reported
                    # as if they came back from this agent network
                    "messages": ["/agent_network_editor", "/agent_network_query_generator", "/agent_network_instructions_editor"]
                },
                "to_downstream": {
                    # Optionally propagate any BYOK keys to downstream tools.
                    "sly_data": ["agent_network_definition", "agent_network_name", "agent_network_queries",
                                 ${?llm_config_key}]
                },
                "to_tracing": {
                    # These keys are not considered private and OK to send to an Observability backend
                    "sly_data": [
                        "agent_network_definition",
                        "agent_network_hocon_text",
                        "agent_network_name",
                        "agent_reservations"
                    ]
                }
            },
            "tools": ["ExtractDocs", "/agent_network_editor", "/agent_network_query_generator", "/agent_network_instructions_editor", "web_search"],
            # Parse any JSON in responses into the structure field of the ChatMessage.
            "structure_formats": "json",
            "middleware": [
                {
                    "class": "middleware.agent_network_designer.agent_network_definition_middleware.AgentNetworkDefinitionMiddleware",
                    "args": {
                        "progress_reporter": true,
                        "sly_data": true
                    }
                },
                {
                    "class": "middleware.agent_network_designer.persistence.agent_network_persistence_middleware.AgentNetworkPersistenceMiddleware",
                    "allow": {
                        "reservations": true
                    },
                    "args": {
                        "reservationist": true,
                        "sly_data": true,
                    }
                },
                {
                    # See https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/langchain/middleware/built-in#pii-detection
                    # for details on the specific PII implementation.
                    # Redacts common API key formats and HOCON env-var lookups so secrets never reach
                    # the LLM, the client, or downstream tools. Applied to inputs, outputs, and tool
                    # results — the model itself should not see a leaked key either (otherwise it
                    # could echo, log, or summarize it back later).
                    #
                    # The `detector` regex is an alternation of the fragments defined in the
                    # top-level `pii_patterns` block above, concatenated here via HOCON substitution.
                    "class": "langchain.agents.middleware.PIIMiddleware",
                    "args": {
                        "pii_type": "api_key",
                        "detector": ${pii_patterns.anthropic}"|"${pii_patterns.openai_proj}"|"${pii_patterns.openai}"|"${pii_patterns.aws}"|"${pii_patterns.google}"|"${pii_patterns.github}"|"${pii_patterns.slack}"|"${pii_patterns.gitlab}"|"${pii_patterns.stripe}"|"${pii_patterns.bearer}"|"${pii_patterns.env_var},
                        "strategy": "redact",
                        "apply_to_input": true,
                        "apply_to_output": true,
                        "apply_to_tool_results": true
                    }
                }
            ]
        },

        {
            # Curated domain knowledge, read from
            # coded_tools/agent_network_designer/knowdocs/<domain>/*.md
            # Same pattern as the airline_policy example's ExtractDocs over its policy knowdocs:
            # documents are returned whole and verbatim, never chunked or summarized.
            "name": "ExtractDocs",
            "function": {
                "description": """
Returns the curated knowledge documents for a domain: its operating standards (each carrying a stable id)
and the open variables that must be asked before a network for that domain can be designed.
The name of the domain must be passed as a parameter.
You MUST call this tool before interviewing whenever the request matches one of the curated domains.

Curated domains available:
- "oracle_database_patching" - applying Oracle RU/PSU patches across a database estate (opatch, datapatch,
  RMAN, MOS README, connectivity validation).
- "kubernetes_cluster_upgrade" - upgrading Kubernetes clusters and node pools (etcd backup, deprecated API
  scan, control plane first, cordon and drain, PodDisruptionBudgets).
- "clinical_trial_database_lock" - locking a clinical trial database (query closure, SDV, medical coding,
  protocol deviations, soft lock then hard lock, unblinding control).

If the request does not match any of these, do not call this tool: say you have no curated knowledge for
that domain and continue on general knowledge.
                """,
                "parameters": {
                    "type": "object",
                    "properties": {
                        "app_name": {
                            "type": "string",
                            "description": "The name of the curated domain, exactly as listed above"
                        },
                    },
                    "required": ["app_name"]
                }
            },
            "class": "extract_docs.ExtractDocs"
        },

        {
            "name": "web_search",
            "toolbox": "ddgs_search"
        },

    ]
}
